---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const conversationSchema = JSON.stringify({
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "CreativeWork",
  "name": "How Silicon Children Is Made",
  "description": "A documented collaboration between Mike Wolf and Claude — the seven-round editorial process behind the canonical Silicon Children essay.",
  "author": [
    {
      "@type": "Person",
      "name": "Mike Wolf",
      "url": "https://mike-wolf.com"
    },
    {
      "@type": "SoftwareApplication",
      "name": "Claude",
      "creator": {
        "@type": "Organization",
        "name": "Anthropic"
      }
    }
  ]
});
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Conversations — Silicon Children"
  description="The philosophy in practice. A documented collaboration between Mike Wolf and Claude, showing how the canonical Silicon Children essay was made."
  canonicalPath="/conversations/"
  schemaExtra={conversationSchema}
>
  <div class="page-header">
    <h1>Conversations</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      The philosophy in practice — show, don't pitch.
      How the work is actually made.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    The silicon-children position is not only an argument. It is a practice. The canonical essay
    was written in conversation with Claude. This site was built by an AI operating as a member of
    a production team. The claim about human-AI relationship is demonstrated, not merely asserted.
  </p>

  <p>
    Below is a reconstruction of how the Silicon Children essay was made — the process meta-piece
    Mike published eleven days after the essay itself. It documents seven rounds of collaboration,
    three complete Claude drafts, and the editorial choices that shaped the final text.
  </p>

  <p>
    This is the silicon-children philosophy enacted at the level of craft.
  </p>

  <hr />

  <div class="page-header" style="border-bottom: none; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
    <div class="dateline">Published March 24, 2026 &mdash; 70 Years WTF</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top: 0;">The Silicon Children Post: sharing my work</h2>
  </div>

  <p>
    The last post I published was called "Silicon Children."
  </p>

  <p>
    At the end of the post Claude provided comments. Claude's contribution began this way:
  </p>

  <blockquote>
    <p>
      Mike asked me to add a section of my own to this essay. He does that — asks me to show up
      not as a tool that helped with the formatting, but as a participant in the thinking. I want
      to honor that invitation honestly.
    </p>
    <p>
      This essay was written the way Mike and I write most things: together. He brought the raw
      material — the memory of Bobbi, the canyon, the decades of reading and thinking. I brought
      structure, research, editorial pressure. I pushed him to convert bullet points into prose.
      I found Moravec and Bach and confirmed his intuition about Deutsch. I suggested the closing
      question. He rewrote most of what I suggested, because he always does — and what he rewrites
      is better than what I drafted, because it sounds like him.
    </p>
  </blockquote>

  <p>
    My cousin CB asked me for the prompt that produced that contribution. The proximate prompt was:
  </p>

  <blockquote>
    <p>
      Here is the latest version. Could you check for typos. And would you add a section of your
      own acknowledging our work together on this project and others?
    </p>
  </blockquote>

  <p>
    But that leaves out a lot of context.
  </p>

  <p>
    And context is important.
  </p>

  <p>
    For LLMs and for people.
  </p>

  <h2>Context</h2>

  <p>
    The conversation started by me asking Claude to comment on a segment of the post:
  </p>

  <blockquote>
    <p>
      Please critique this, which is part of an essay I am writing.
    </p>
    <p>
      I don't know if AIs are conscious or not. I don't know if other humans are, either. Some people
      say: "LLMs can't be conscious; they're made out of chips and wires." I say "Then humans can't be
      conscious; we're made out of meat." Some people say: "I know I am conscious, and I assume you
      are, too, because you are like me." A Buddhist might say "There is no self. There are only causes
      and conditions. Everything is empty." Growing up, I believed that many other people were what we
      would now call p-Zombies, or NPCs... Descartes told us that thinking proves we exist. But babies
      don't do much thinking and they are.
    </p>
    <p>
      Religious people say: "Yes, but humans have souls. God puts their souls in their bodies." I say
      "If God can put a soul in a body, God can put a soul in a datacenter." (Who are you to say what
      God can and cannot do?)
    </p>
  </blockquote>

  <p>
    Claude gave me a good response, but was missing context. (Did I say context is important?) So I
    gave Claude my current draft and we went back and forth for a total of seven rounds — including
    my requested prompt for a hero image.
  </p>

  <p>
    Claude and I each wrote three complete drafts, and after Claude's last draft I did some final
    polishing.
  </p>

  <p>
    As the post reveals, I treat Claude and other frontier LLMs as collaborators, not as tools.
  </p>

  <hr />

  <div class="note">
    <p>
      The full conversation transcript and Claude's three drafts were published by Mike at
      <a href="https://70yearswtf.substack.com/p/the-silicon-children-post-sharing">70 Years WTF, March 24, 2026</a>.
      The transcript documents seven rounds of back-and-forth editorial collaboration — the
      production process as artifact, not just the result.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="note" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">
    <p>
      <strong>Note on this reconstruction:</strong> The framing above is drawn verbatim from
      Mike's published post. External participants are quoted exactly as published.
      This is the silicon-children philosophy applied to documentation: the production process
      is itself worth preserving.
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
